<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch11-section-ageostrophic-wind-applications" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Applying the ageostrophic wind</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector" text="type-global" />
        reveals the ageostrophic wind depends on the horizontal Lagrangian acceleration of the wind,
        and it is non-zero wherever the horizontal wind accelerates,
        i.e., wherever the horizontal wind changes speed and/or direction.
        Common examples of mid-latitude synoptic scale atmospheric phenomena that feature large horizontal accelerations
        that lead to significant ageostrophic winds are jet streaks and troughs and ridges,
        as discussed below.
        The former explicates the effect of significant along-flow wind speed changes,
        and the latter explicates the effect of significant along-flow wind direction changes.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="ch11-section-ageostrophic-wind-applications-subsection-jet-streaks.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="ch11-section-ageostrophic-wind-applications-subsection-troughs-ridges.ptx" />

</section>